==Russian==

===Etymology 1===
From {{proto|Slavic|gradъ|lang=ru}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|greh₃d-|lang=ru}}. Cognate with Lithuanian [[grúodas]] (Balto-Slavic *grōda-), Latin {{term|grando|grandō|lang=la}}, Armenian {{term|կարկուտ|sc=Armn|lang=hy|tr=karkut}} (from Old Armenian *gagrōdo-), Old Indic *hrādúniṣ, and possibly with English {{term|grind}}.

====Noun====
'''град''' (grad) {{m}}

# {{meteorology|lang=ru}} [[hail]]
#:* Град [[идти|идёт]] — ''It’s hailing.''
# {{meteorology|lang=ru}} [[shower]], [[rain]]
# [[volley]], [[shower]]
#:* град [[пуля|пуль]] — ''hail of bullets''

=====Declension=====
{{ru-noun1|град||гра́да||гра́ду||град||гра́дом||о гра́де|}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{proto|Slavic|gordъ|settlement, enclosed place|lang=ru}}, from {{proto|Balto-Slavic|gordos|lang=ru}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|gʰordʰ-o-|lang=ru}}, either directly or possibly via {{proto|Germanic|garda}}. Compare {{etyl|cu|ru}} {{term|градъ||town|sc=Cyrs|tr=grádŭ|lang=cu}}. Cognate with English {{term|garden}}, {{term|yard}}, {{term|gird}}, German {{term|Garten|lang=de}}, Danish {{term|gård|lang=da}}, Norwegian {{term|gard|lang=no}}, Lithuanian {{term|gardas||garden|lang=lt}}, Sanskrit {{term|गृह |tr=gṛha|sc=Deva||house, home|lang=sa}}, Gothic {{term|𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃|lang=got|sc=Goth|tr=gards}}.

====Noun====
{{infl|ru|noun|tr=grad|m}}

# {{context|pоetic|antiquated}} [[town]], [[city]]

=====Declension=====
{{ru-noun1|гра́д|гра́ды|гра́да|гра́дов|гра́ду|гра́дам|гра́д|гра́ды|гра́дом|гра́дами|о гра́де|о гра́дах}}

=====Related terms=====
* [[город]]

===Etymology 3===

====Abbreviation====
'''град.''' ([[градус]], ''grádus'')

# [[degree]]

